---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
// The accessibility-fallback copy (eyebrow / headline / lede) lives in
// src/data/home.json (plain file edits). Rendered with set:html
// so inline emphasis (<em>/<strong>) and entities (&nbsp;) render as authored.
// (The live map + marketing intro were retired from the front door by ADR-0148 §5;
// the how-it-works page that hosted the full TreeWorld map was itself retired in
// the info-pages retirement — the experience is now the whole site.)
import home from '../data/home.json';
import snapshotJson from '../data/forest-snapshot.json';
// The Act 2 guided walk (ADR-0145) renders a buildScene → sceneToSvg map SVG that
// needs the `tw-*` map-layer paint rules; the walk is now their SOLE consumer (the
// TreeWorld component that used to share them retired with how-it-works). Without
// this stylesheet the territory/flora would fall back to black fill.
import '../styles/tree-world-map.css';
// GROW (ADR-0453 D11): chapter 2 opens on storytree's REAL forest, from the same stamped snapshot
// `/forest/` renders, laid out and serialised HERE at build time. A synthetic forest is a claim
// about a product; the real one is evidence of it, and this site's whole argument is that the
// system built it.
//
// ⚠ THIS IMPORT IS BUILD-TIME ONLY, and there is a live hazard sitting under it that the next
// person to touch this line needs to know about.
//
// Astro frontmatter runs at BUILD time: the map is serialised to markup here and the browser
// downloads no part of this module graph. Measured on the built output (2026-08-28, after TELL):
// 34.8 KB of client JS across two chunks, with no `zod`, no `act2-director` and no `three` in
// either. That is the property that actually matters and it holds.
//
// ⚠ READ THAT AS TWO NUMBERS, NOT ONE. Only 20.8 KB is on the ENTRY path — the single `<script
// type="module">` in the built HTML. The other 14 KB is `inflection`, which chapter 2 fetches at
// the transform click and which nothing references statically, so a visitor who never presses "show
// me the better way" downloads none of it. TELL's prose, its state machine and its loop diagram all
// live in that lazy chunk (grep the built output: "This is storytree" appears in `inflection.*.js`
// and not in the entry chunk). Anything added to chapter 2 should land there too; the way to check
// is that grep, not the total.
//
// The one wrinkle: `forest-snapshot-map` reaches `act2-walkthrough` for its disc geometry, and
// `act2-walkthrough` imports `../lib/forest-world-r3f/act2-director` — a specifier
// `check:web-experience-closure` (ADR-0336) forbids in this page's CLIENT closure. It is reported as
// a build-time NOTE rather than a finding, because this import is frontmatter: the rung classifies
// build-time reach separately and only fails on a client seed.
//
// ⚠ TWO CLAIMS THAT USED TO STAND HERE ARE NOW FALSE, and both were reassuring in the wrong
// direction. (1) This comment said the rung passed "ONLY because its `STATIC_IMPORT_RE` excludes
// newlines" and could not see multi-line imports — that blindness was REPAIRED on 2026-08-28 (the
// character class no longer excludes `\n`), so reflowing an import in `act2-walkthrough.ts` is no
// longer a way to red the gate for free. (2) It said `website-refresh-arc-pitch-overlays` was
// "about to retire" `act2-walkthrough`. TELL did NOT: the walkthrough is the scripted three-story
// WALK, not the narrator, and the geometry above still draws the real forest that ships today.
// What TELL retired is `act2-orchestrator` and its chrome.
//
// So the extraction is still owed and still worth doing — lift `buildDisc`/`escXml` out of
// `act2-walkthrough` into their own module and this page stops reaching an R3F specifier at all —
// but it is nobody's incidental cleanup, and it must not be written down again as something the
// next increment will absorb.
import { assertSnapshot, forestArrivalSvg, renderStamp } from '../scripts/forest-snapshot-map';
const forestSnapshot = assertSnapshot(snapshotJson);
const forestArrival = forestArrivalSvg(forestSnapshot);
const forestStamp = renderStamp(forestSnapshot);
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE HOME FLIP (ADR-0134 Act 1): the page ships two top-level branches.
//   1. The storm  — [data-experience-entry] — a diegetic AI-agent terminal
//      storm, hidden by default and armed before paint by the inline script
//      below ONLY when JS is available and the visitor has no reduced-motion
//      preference. Plain DOM/CSS + one 2D grain canvas + Web Audio synthesis;
//      no WebGL anywhere in this page's static import closure.
//   2. The calm view — [data-experience-fallback] — today's home content,
//      exactly: no-JS visitors, reduced-motion visitors, and anyone who skips
//      or steps out of the storm land here. The storm is a choice, not a toll
//      booth.
// The skip control [data-experience-skip] is persistent from boot; all three
// markers are machine-held by the parent repo's `check:web-experience`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
import { BOOT_BANNER, PROMPT_CHIPS, DEFAULT_PROMPT } from '../scripts/storm-script';
---

<Base active="home">
  <!-- Arm the storm BEFORE first paint (is:inline — Astro must not defer this):
       calm by default; enhance to storm only for motion-OK, JS-on visitors.
       The disarm path (skip / Escape / the land's classic affordance) lives here too, so the
       exit works even if the storm engine module never loads. -->
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var de = document.documentElement;
      try {
        if (!window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
          de.classList.add('storm-armed');
        }
      } catch (e) {
        /* no matchMedia → stay calm */
      }
      function disarm() {
        if (!de.classList.contains('storm-armed')) return;
        de.classList.remove('storm-armed');
        try {
          if (window.__stormHalt) window.__stormHalt();
        } catch (e) {
          /* the engine may never have loaded — the calm view stands anyway */
        }
        window.scrollTo(0, 0);
        var calm = document.getElementById('calm-view');
        if (calm) {
          try {
            calm.focus({ preventScroll: true });
          } catch (e) {
            calm.focus();
          }
        }
      }
      document.addEventListener('click', function (ev) {
        var t = ev.target && ev.target.closest ? ev.target.closest('[data-storm-disarm]') : null;
        if (!t) return;
        ev.preventDefault();
        // ADR-0148 §5: the top-left "show me a better way" skip jumps a capable
        // (JS) visitor STRAIGHT into the 2.5D tutorial — the tutorial is the front
        // door, not the static page. If the engine has not loaded (or cannot),
        // fall through to the calm fallback so the exit is never dead.
        if (t.hasAttribute('data-experience-skip') && window.__stormSkipToTutorial) {
          try {
            window.__stormSkipToTutorial();
            return;
          } catch (e) {
            /* engine present but threw — degrade to the calm view below */
          }
        }
        disarm();
      });
      document.addEventListener('keydown', function (ev) {
        if (ev.key === 'Escape') disarm();
      });
    })();
  </script>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── the storm ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section
    class="storm-root"
    data-experience-entry
    aria-label="A staged demo: one prompt spawns a swarm of AI coding agents. Skip any time."
  >
    <div class="storm-hud">
      <a class="storm-skip" href="#calm-view" data-experience-skip data-storm-disarm
        >show me a better way →</a
      >
      <span class="storm-count" id="storm-count" aria-hidden="true">AGENTS: 01</span>
      <button class="storm-sound" id="storm-sound" type="button" aria-pressed="true">
        SOUND ON
      </button>
    </div>

    <div class="storm-stage" id="storm-stage">
      <!-- the boot terminal: already "logged in", prompt ready, silent -->
      <div class="storm-term storm-boot ph-green" id="storm-boot">
        <div class="term-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="term-dots">● ● ●</span>
          <span class="term-name">swarm — ~/app</span>
          <span class="term-status" id="storm-boot-status">idle</span>
        </div>
        <div class="term-body" id="storm-boot-body" aria-hidden="true">
          {BOOT_BANNER.map((l) => <div class="term-line k-banner">{l}</div>)}
        </div>
        <div class="term-chips" id="storm-chips" role="group" aria-label="Suggested tasks">
          {PROMPT_CHIPS.map((c) => (
            <button type="button" class="term-chip" data-chip>
              {c}
            </button>
          ))}
        </div>
        <form class="term-prompt" id="storm-form">
          <label class="storm-sr" for="storm-input">Give the coding agent a task</label>
          <span class="term-ps1" aria-hidden="true">~/app $</span>
          <input
            id="storm-input"
            name="prompt"
            type="text"
            autocomplete="off"
            spellcheck="false"
            maxlength="120"
            value={DEFAULT_PROMPT}
            placeholder="or type your own task…"
          />
          <button type="submit" class="term-send">run</button>
        </form>
      </div>

      <!-- the spawned sub-agent terminals materialize here (streaming noise —
           hidden from assistive tech; the exits above stay in the tree) -->
      <div class="storm-spawn" id="storm-spawn" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- ONE full-viewport grain pass: animated noise on the canvas, scanlines +
         vignette as its CSS background — never per-terminal filters -->
    <canvas class="storm-grain" id="storm-grain" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

    <div class="storm-dimmer" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <!-- at peak: the root agent's finale terminal drops in above the dimmed
         storm and concedes the obvious. Its dialogue streams from the seeded
         plan (storm-script.ts); its primary button is THE TRANSFORM trigger
         (ADR-0134 §2): the engine binds [data-storm-transform] — one click and
         the storm becomes soil, the calm land fades up in place. The ghost
         link is the joke exit: if a wall of terminals is genuinely your thing,
         that product exists. This window holds the page's real controls, so it
         stays in the accessibility tree (only the streamed noise is hidden). -->
    <div
      class="storm-term storm-finale ph-green"
      id="storm-finale"
      role="group"
      aria-label="The lead agent concedes the swarm of terminals is not working and offers to show a better way"
      hidden
    >
      <div class="term-bar" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="term-dots">● ● ●</span>
        <span class="term-name">swarm — root</span>
        <span class="term-status" id="storm-finale-status">reporting</span>
      </div>
      <div class="term-body" id="storm-finale-body" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="finale-actions" id="storm-finale-actions" hidden>
        <button type="button" class="finale-btn finale-btn--primary" data-storm-transform>
          show me the better way →
        </button>
        <a class="finale-btn finale-btn--ghost" href="https://claude.com/product/claude-code">
          i'm fine with this
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- the soil: the terminals' fragments accumulate here as they fall — the
         noise literally becomes the ground of the calm world. -->
    <div class="storm-soil" id="storm-soil" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <!-- the calm land (ADR-0148 §5): the transform lands the visitor STRAIGHT
         in the 2.5D guided tutorial — no R3F 3D island, no "begin the guided
         walk" second click, no classic-front-page detour. The tutorial module
         is only ever reached via dynamic import() at the transform click and is
         pure SVG/DOM (zero WebGL). Hidden until the transform begins; the walk
         stage + the orchestrator's mock-website proposal mount onto this layer.
         Escape / the storm skip stay the never-stranded exit to the fallback. -->
    <div
      class="storm-land"
      id="storm-land"
      tabindex="-1"
      aria-label="storytree's own forest — every island a microservice. Drag to move around it."
      hidden
    >
      <div class="storm-land-canvas" id="storm-land-canvas" set:html={forestArrival}></div>
      <!-- The stamp is load-bearing copy, not a caption: it is what stops a snapshot reading as a
           live view, and the site's own `signals-must-be-real` principle is the reason. Rendered
           in the markup rather than by script so it is present even if the framing script never
           runs — an undated map is the one way this backfires. -->
      <p class="storm-land-stamp">{forestStamp}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────── the accessibility fallback (ADR-0148 §5) ───────────────────
       A CLEAN MINIMAL static page — NOT the old marketing homepage, and NOT an
       escape a capable visitor is offered. It is graceful degradation only: no-JS
       and reduced-motion visitors land here (and the storm's skip / Escape route
       here too), never stranded. `data-experience-fallback`
       stays physically in this source (the parent's check:web-experience greps for
       it, and for data-experience-skip in the storm HUD above). The headline copy
       lives in home.json. -->
  <div class="calm-view" id="calm-view" tabindex="-1" data-experience-fallback>
    <section class="fallback section">
      <div class="wrap fallback__copy">
        <p class="eyebrow" set:html={home.eyebrow} />
        <h1 set:html={home.headline} />
        <p class="lede" set:html={home.lede} />
        <p class="fallback__note">
          storytree grows software as a living map of the system, watched as it happens. This is
          the plain-text door; the full guided experience needs JavaScript and motion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<!-- the storm engine (module: deferred, bundled). If it fails for any reason,
     fall back to the calm view — never a dead storm. -->
<script>
  import { runStorm } from '../scripts/act1-storm';
  if (document.documentElement.classList.contains('storm-armed')) {
    try {
      runStorm();
    } catch (err) {
      console.error('storm engine failed — falling back to the calm view', err);
      document.documentElement.classList.remove('storm-armed');
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* ---- the accessibility fallback (ADR-0148 §5): a clean minimal static page,
         graceful degradation only ---- */
  #calm-view:focus {
    outline: none;
  }
  .fallback {
    padding-top: clamp(2.8rem, 8vw, 5.5rem);
    padding-bottom: clamp(1.4rem, 4vw, 2.6rem);
  }
  .fallback__copy { max-width: 42rem; }
  .fallback__copy h1 { margin-top: 0.2rem; margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  /* :global so emphasis authored in the editable copy (rendered via set:html,
     which Astro does not scope) still picks up the italic styling. */
  .fallback :global(em) { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink); }
  .fallback__note {
    margin: 1rem 0 0; max-width: 38rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .fallback__cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
</style>

<style is:global>
  /* ======================================================================
     Act 1 — the terminal storm. Global (not Astro-scoped) because the
     sub-agent terminals are created by the engine at runtime. Everything
     is namespaced storm-/term- to stay out of the site's way.
     ====================================================================== */

  /* ---- arming: calm by default, storm only under html.storm-armed ---- */
  .storm-root { display: none; }
  html.storm-armed .storm-root { display: block; }
  html.storm-armed .nav,
  html.storm-armed .foot,
  html.storm-armed .calm-view { display: none; }
  html.storm-armed body { background: #060a08; overflow: hidden; }

  /* ---- the room: dark phosphor, the deliberate inverse of the cream calm ---- */
  .storm-root {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 60;
    background: radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 30%, #0b1410 0%, #060a08 55%, #030504 100%);
    color: #9fe8b4;
    --font-term: ui-monospace, 'Cascadia Mono', 'SF Mono', Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-family: var(--font-term);
  }

  .storm-sr {
    position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; padding: 0; margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; border: 0;
  }

  /* ---- HUD: the skip (persistent from boot), the arcade counter, sound ---- */
  .storm-hud {
    position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; z-index: 48;
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    font: 600 11.5px/1 var(--font-term);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }
  .storm-skip {
    color: #d8ffe4; text-decoration: none; text-transform: lowercase;
    border: 1px solid rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.4);
    background: rgba(6, 14, 9, 0.72);
    padding: 8px 13px; border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .storm-skip:hover { background: rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.14); color: #ffffff; }
  .storm-count {
    margin-left: auto; display: inline-block;
    color: #baffcf; font-size: 13px;
    text-shadow: 0 0 9px rgba(90, 255, 140, 0.55);
  }
  .storm-count.tick { animation: storm-count-pulse 0.5s ease-out; }
  @keyframes storm-count-pulse {
    0% { transform: scale(1.3); filter: brightness(2.2); }
    45% { transform: scale(0.97); }
    100% { transform: scale(1); filter: brightness(1); }
  }
  .storm-sound {
    font: 600 10.5px/1 var(--font-term); letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #9fe8b4; background: rgba(6, 14, 9, 0.72);
    border: 1px solid rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.28);
    padding: 8px 11px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .storm-sound[aria-pressed='false'] { color: #57806a; text-decoration: line-through; }
  .storm-sound:hover { background: rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.12); }

  /* ---- stage + terminals ---- */
  .storm-stage { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 10; overflow: hidden; }
  .storm-spawn { position: absolute; inset: 0; }

  .storm-term {
    position: absolute;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; overflow: hidden;
    background: rgba(7, 16, 11, 0.93);
    border: 1px solid rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.26);
    border-radius: 6px;
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6),
      0 14px 34px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55),
      0 0 22px rgba(80, 255, 140, 0.07);
    --ph: #8affa8; --ph-soft: #b8ffcb; --ph-dim: #4e8d63;
  }
  .storm-term.ph-amber {
    --ph: #ffc46b; --ph-soft: #ffe0a8; --ph-dim: #a67c3e;
    border-color: rgba(255, 196, 107, 0.28);
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6),
      0 14px 34px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55),
      0 0 22px rgba(255, 180, 80, 0.07);
  }
  /* CRT power-on (individual scale so it composes with the mobile translate) */
  .storm-term.is-new { animation: storm-term-on 0.34s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1.5, 0.36, 1) both; }
  @keyframes storm-term-on {
    0% { scale: 1 0.05; filter: brightness(3); opacity: 0; }
    40% { scale: 1.03 0.55; filter: brightness(1.9); opacity: 1; }
    100% { scale: 1 1; filter: brightness(1); }
  }

  .term-bar {
    flex: none;
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;
    padding: 5px 9px;
    background: rgba(10, 24, 16, 0.9);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.14);
    font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ph-dim);
  }
  .term-dots { font-size: 7px; letter-spacing: 2px; color: rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.4); }
  .term-name { color: var(--ph-soft); font-weight: 600; }
  .term-status { margin-left: auto; }
  .storm-term.is-parked .term-status { color: var(--ph); animation: storm-status-blink 1.4s steps(1) infinite; }
  @keyframes storm-status-blink { 50% { opacity: 0.25; } }

  .term-body {
    flex: 1 1 auto; min-height: 0;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: flex-end;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: 8px 10px 9px;
    font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.5;
    text-shadow: 0 0 7px rgba(120, 255, 160, 0.28);
  }
  .term-line { white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: break-word; color: var(--ph); }
  .term-line.k-banner { color: var(--ph-dim); }
  .term-line.k-sys { color: var(--ph-dim); font-style: italic; }
  .term-line.k-cmd { color: #eafff0; font-weight: 600; }
  .term-line.k-warn { color: #ff9d76; }
  .term-line.k-ok { color: var(--ph-soft); }
  .term-line.k-spawn { color: #eafff0; font-weight: 700; }
  .term-line.k-demand {
    align-self: flex-start;
    background: var(--ph); color: #071009;
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 1px 7px; margin-top: 5px; border-radius: 2px;
    text-shadow: none;
  }
  .term-cursor { animation: storm-cur-blink 1.05s steps(1) infinite; }
  @keyframes storm-cur-blink { 50% { opacity: 0; } }

  /* ---- the boot terminal ---- */
  .storm-boot {
    left: 50%; top: 42%; translate: -50% -50%;
    width: min(620px, 92vw);
    z-index: 15;
  }
  .storm-boot .term-body { max-height: 42vh; font-size: 13px; }
  .term-chips { flex: none; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 7px; padding: 6px 10px 2px; }
  .term-chip {
    font: 500 11.5px var(--font-term);
    color: var(--ph-soft);
    background: rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.08);
    border: 1px solid rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.32);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 5px 9px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .term-chip:hover { background: rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.16); }
  .term-prompt { flex: none; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 7px; padding: 7px 10px 11px; }
  .term-ps1 { color: var(--ph-dim); font-size: 13px; }
  .term-prompt input {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0;
    background: transparent; border: 0; outline: none;
    color: #eafff0; font: 500 13px var(--font-term);
    caret-color: var(--ph);
  }
  .term-prompt input::placeholder { color: rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.4); }
  .term-prompt input:focus-visible { outline: none; box-shadow: 0 1px 0 0 var(--ph); }
  .term-send {
    font: 600 10.5px var(--font-term); letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #071009; background: var(--ph);
    border: 0; border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 5px 10px; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .storm-root.is-sent .term-chips,
  .storm-root.is-sent .term-prompt { display: none; }

  /* ---- grain: animated noise (canvas) + scanlines & vignette (its background) ---- */
  .storm-grain {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 40;
    width: 100%; height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    background:
      repeating-linear-gradient(
        0deg,
        rgba(4, 10, 6, 0.28) 0px, rgba(4, 10, 6, 0.28) 1px,
        transparent 1px, transparent 3px
      ),
      radial-gradient(ellipse 115% 90% at 50% 42%, transparent 55%, rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5) 100%);
    animation: storm-flicker 7s steps(60) infinite;
  }
  @keyframes storm-flicker {
    0%, 91%, 94%, 96%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
    92% { opacity: 0.86; }
    97% { opacity: 0.93; }
  }

  /* ---- peak: dim the scene, the root agent's finale terminal drops in ---- */
  .storm-dimmer {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 45;
    background: rgba(2, 4, 3, 0.55);
    opacity: 0; transition: opacity 1.6s ease;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .storm-root.is-peak .storm-dimmer { opacity: 1; }
  /* the finale terminal: same CRT species as the storm windows, just bigger,
     centered above the dimmer (dimmer z 45). CRT power-on entrance reuses
     storm-term-on — `scale` composes with the centering translate. */
  .storm-finale {
    left: 50%; top: 50%; translate: -50% -50%;
    z-index: 46;
    width: min(640px, 92vw);
    max-height: min(70vh, 560px);
  }
  .storm-finale[hidden] { display: none; }
  .storm-root.is-peak .storm-finale {
    animation: storm-term-on 0.42s 0.9s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1.5, 0.36, 1) both;
  }
  .storm-finale .term-body {
    min-height: 200px;
    max-height: 46vh;
    font-size: 13px;
  }
  /* the two options: hidden until the stream lands, then a soft fade-up */
  .finale-actions {
    flex: none;
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 10px;
    padding: 12px 14px 14px;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.14);
  }
  .finale-actions[hidden] { display: none; }
  .storm-finale.is-done .finale-actions { animation: finale-actions-in 0.7s ease both; }
  @keyframes finale-actions-in {
    from { opacity: 0; translate: 0 7px; }
    to { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }
  }
  .finale-btn {
    font: 600 12px var(--font-term);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: lowercase;
    border-radius: 4px; padding: 9px 14px;
    cursor: pointer; text-decoration: none;
  }
  .finale-btn--primary { color: #071009; background: var(--ph); border: 0; }
  .finale-btn--primary:hover { filter: brightness(1.15); }
  .finale-btn--ghost {
    color: #6fa585; background: transparent;
    border: 1px solid rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.22);
  }
  .finale-btn--ghost:hover { color: #9fe8b4; background: rgba(126, 255, 165, 0.08); }

  /* ======================================================================
     The inflection (ADR-0134 §2) — one click at peak transforms the storm:
     terminals power off, their fragments fall as soil, the calm land fades
     up in place. State classes (engine-driven): .is-transforming from the
     click, .is-resolved when the island is mounted and the beat has passed.
     ====================================================================== */

  /* the chrome exhales: grain and HUD fade out (animations are cancelled so
     the opacity transition owns the property); the finale terminal joins the
     collapse with the same CRT power-off as the storm windows (later in the
     cascade than the is-peak power-on, so it wins while both classes stand) */
  .storm-root.is-transforming .storm-grain {
    animation: none;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 1.3s ease;
  }
  /* Once the storm transforms into the tutorial the skip/disarm HUD retires
     entirely — go all in on Act 2 (ADR-0148 §5): fade it out, then flip it
     `visibility: hidden` so it is not just invisible but fully inert (no mouse
     click, out of the tab order) — no capable-visitor escape to the fallback.
     The `data-experience-skip` marker stays in the SOURCE, so check:web-experience
     (which greps the page text) is unaffected; this is runtime-only. */
  .storm-root.is-transforming .storm-hud {
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    transition: opacity 1.5s ease 0.3s, visibility 0s linear 1.8s;
  }
  .storm-root.is-transforming .storm-finale {
    animation: storm-term-off 0.55s 0.3s cubic-bezier(0.55, 0, 0.75, 0.4) both;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* CRT power-off — the inverse of storm-term-on (scale composes with the
     mobile translate the same way) */
  .storm-term.is-off {
    animation: storm-term-off 0.5s cubic-bezier(0.55, 0, 0.75, 0.4) both;
  }
  @keyframes storm-term-off {
    0% { scale: 1 1; filter: brightness(1); opacity: 1; }
    30% { scale: 1.02 0.55; filter: brightness(2.6); opacity: 1; }
    100% { scale: 1 0.04; filter: brightness(3); opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* falling fragments: phosphor text dropping out of the dying windows.
     transform/opacity only — the engine animates each span via WAAPI. */
  .storm-fall {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 47;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .storm-frag {
    position: absolute;
    font: 500 12px var(--font-term);
    white-space: nowrap;
    will-change: transform, opacity;
    color: #8affa8;
    text-shadow: 0 0 7px rgba(120, 255, 160, 0.35);
  }
  .storm-frag.ph-amber {
    color: #ffc46b;
    text-shadow: 0 0 7px rgba(255, 180, 80, 0.35);
  }

  /* the soil: a low dark mound the fragments accumulate into. Grows by an
     engine-set scaleY (transform-only — compositor-friendly); rests as the
     loading posture if the island fetch is slow. */
  .storm-soil {
    position: absolute; left: -4vw; right: -4vw; bottom: 0; z-index: 46;
    height: 13vh;
    background: radial-gradient(120% 100% at 50% 100%, #182419 0%, #0d1510 55%, #070c09 100%);
    border-radius: 48% 48% 0 0 / 92% 92% 0 0;
    transform: scaleY(0.045);
    transform-origin: bottom;
    transition: transform 0.5s cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.7, 0.4, 1), opacity 0.9s ease;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .storm-root.is-transforming .storm-soil { opacity: 1; }

  /* the skip-straight-to-tutorial jump (ADR-0148 §5): the top-left "show me a
     better way" crosses straight to the tutorial. Unlike the finale transform,
     there is no storm to collapse — so suppress the soil mound and fade any live
     terminals out fast, for a clean cross-dissolve into the land, not a collapse. */
  .storm-root.is-transforming.is-skip .storm-soil { opacity: 0; }
  .storm-root.is-transforming.is-skip .storm-term { opacity: 0; transition: opacity 0.4s ease; }

  /* the calm land: mounted hidden→opacity-0, then fades up as the quiet
     resolves. Sits above all storm chrome (runtime hiding of the skip after
     the transform — its source attribute is untouched). */
  .storm-land {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 50;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    outline: none;
    transition: opacity 2.4s ease;
  }
  .storm-land[hidden] { display: none; }
  .storm-root.is-resolved .storm-land { opacity: 1; pointer-events: auto; }
  .storm-land-canvas { position: absolute; inset: 0; }

  /* GROW: the real forest fills the layer, and the SVG's own viewBox does the framing — the
     client script sets it to the designed resting view (ADR-0471) on mount and on resize.
     `touch-action: none` is what makes drag-to-pan work on a touch screen: without it the
     browser claims the gesture for page scrolling and the pointermove handler never sees it. */
  .storm-land-canvas .forest-arrival-svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    touch-action: none;
    cursor: grab;
  }
  /* ⚠ THE BOARD COLOUR IS ON THE LAYER, NOT ONLY ON THE MAP'S OWN BACKGROUND RECT. The SVG's
     `tw-bg` rect covers the WORLD, and the view is routinely larger than the world — whenever the
     frame is wider than the forest at the resting scale, and always once the reader zooms out past
     the fit. Without this, those margins show the dark storm behind, and the forest reads as a
     letterboxed picture pasted onto the previous chapter instead of as ground the visitor is
     standing on. Same two stops as the map's own `#tw-board` gradient. */
  .storm-land-canvas {
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse 80% 80% at 50% 40%, #fbf3ea 0%, #edd9c9 100%);
  }
  .storm-land-canvas .forest-arrival-svg.is-grabbing { cursor: grabbing; }
  /* The dependency trails are hidden by default in the shared stylesheet (each host opts in).
     Here they are quiet connective tissue — what depends on what, readable but never competing
     with the islands. Same treatment as the /forest/ poster. */
  .storm-land-canvas .tw-trails { display: inline; }
  .storm-land-canvas .tw-trail-shadow,
  .storm-land-canvas .tw-trail-casing { display: none; }
  .storm-land-canvas .tw-trail-fill { stroke: rgba(122, 98, 62, 0.42); stroke-width: 3; }

  /* The snapshot stamp. Sits over the map rather than under it because the map fills the
     viewport, and it must be readable without scrolling: a map that presents itself as a live
     reading is the single way this backfires. */
  .storm-land-stamp {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    bottom: 1.1rem;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    z-index: 2;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: min(46rem, calc(100% - 2rem));
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: #4a3a2a;
    background: rgba(251, 243, 234, 0.82);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.9rem;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .storm-land-stamp { font-size: 0.74rem; bottom: 0.6rem; }
  }

  /* ======================================================================
     TELL — the prose that phases in over the settled forest, and the one
     diagram it is allowed. Global because act2-tell.ts creates all of it at
     runtime; namespaced tell-. Mounted onto the #storm-land layer, so it
     shares the land's fade-up and its disarm path.

     ⚠ THE LAYER IS pointer-events: none EXCEPT ITS SKIP BUTTON, and that is
     load-bearing rather than tidy: the map underneath stays draggable for the
     whole sequence, and touching it ENDS the sequence. TELL is something the
     visitor is shown, never something they are held inside — which is the
     difference between this and the chat dock it replaced, whose only exit was
     to keep agreeing with it.

     What follows the overlay is the COMPACT loop-diagram skin for
     buildLoopDiagram (act2-loop-diagram.ts) — the honest TDD LOOP (ADR-0157
     §5): boxes + arrows that visibly loop. The WRITE nodes read as the agents;
     the CHECK nodes read as the SYSTEM (the referee) — the load-bearing honest
     distinction, and the one thing the forest structurally cannot show.
     ====================================================================== */

  .tell-layer {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 8;
    pointer-events: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    padding: clamp(1.6rem, 5vh, 3.2rem) clamp(1.2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    transition: opacity 0.85s ease;
  }
  .tell-layer.is-over { opacity: 0; }

  /* The prose column. Narrow on purpose — a measure this short is read in one
     eye movement per line, which is what lets a sentence land while the reader
     is still mostly looking at the map. */
  .tell-column {
    max-width: min(30rem, 62vw);
    /* A soft scrim rather than a panel: the copy has to stay legible over both
       the cream board and a dark island crown, and a hard card would read as a
       dialog the visitor has to dismiss. */
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 0 rgba(251, 243, 234, 0.85),
      0 0 18px rgba(251, 243, 234, 0.95),
      0 0 34px rgba(251, 243, 234, 0.7);
  }
  /* ======================================================================
     THE FOREST ARRIVES BY GROWING (forest-growth.ts) — chapter 2's GROW.

     ⚠ EVERY RULE HERE IS SCOPED UNDER `.forest-arrival-svg.is-growing`, AND
     THAT SCOPE IS THE NO-SCRIPT GUARANTEE, NOT TIDINESS. The class is added
     by `mountForestGrowth` at runtime. A visitor whose script never runs
     therefore matches none of this and gets the whole forest as the static,
     dated picture the build serialised — the property `…-arc-arrival` bought
     and this must not quietly spend. Hoisting `opacity: 0` out of this scope
     would hide the map from exactly the people who cannot tell you.

     THE ORDER FOLLOWS THE DEPENDENCY EDGE (ADR-0491). Base islands form from
     nothing; a settled island releases its outgoing pathways; a pathway
     arriving is what starts the island it reaches. Every window — island,
     flora, and each trail segment's own draw-on — is stamped as a custom
     property by `mountForestGrowth`, so this stylesheet holds the LOOK and
     the schedule holds the ORDER. There are no wave constants here any more.
     ====================================================================== */
  @keyframes act2-forest-grow {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.46); translate: 0 7px; }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1);    translate: 0 0; }
  }
  @keyframes act2-forest-rise {
    from { opacity: 0; translate: 0 9px; }
    to   { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }
  }
  /* THE ROAD DRAWS FROM THE ISLAND IT LEAVES. `pathLength="1"` is stamped by
     the script, so one keyframe reveals a segment of any real length; the
     reversed variant is for a chain that walks a segment against the
     direction it was drawn in, and grows from the far end instead. */
  @keyframes act2-trail-draw {
    from { stroke-dashoffset: 1; }
    to   { stroke-dashoffset: 0; }
  }
  @keyframes act2-trail-draw-rev {
    from { stroke-dashoffset: -1; }
    to   { stroke-dashoffset: 0; }
  }
  /* THE LAND. `tw-isle` (the shore) and `tw-ground` (the disc) share a bounding
     box — their centres agree within three user units — so a 50%/50% fill-box
     origin scales both about the island's real centre and they stay registered
     to each other for the whole growth. ⚠ A BOTTOM-EDGE ORIGIN IS THE OBVIOUS
     WAY TO WRITE "grows from its base" AND IT IS THE ONE THAT BREAKS: the two
     boxes share a centre, not a bottom (the shore extends past the disc), so
     100% would scale them about points tens of units apart and slide the land
     around inside its own coastline mid-arrival. The rise is a TRANSLATE,
     which is bounding-box independent and therefore exact for both.

     ⚠ AND THE EASING NO LONGER OVERSHOOTS. The previous curve ended at 1.32
     before settling, which is what the owner was describing as islands
     "poping up" — an overshoot reads as something landing, not as something
     growing. This one is monotone. */
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-isle,
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-ground {
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: 50% 50%;
    animation: act2-forest-grow var(--grow-dur, 760ms) cubic-bezier(0.16, 0.72, 0.28, 1) both;
    animation-delay: var(--grow-delay, 0ms);
  }
  /* THE TREES. ⚠ DELIBERATELY NOT SCALED — see forest-growth.ts. `tw-terr`
     wraps the trees AND the name plate below them, so where the disc sits
     inside its box varies per island (measured: 28.7%–50.4% vertically). Any
     fixed origin slides the trees off their own ground, worst on the tall
     islands. A translate is bounding-box independent and therefore exact. */
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-terr {
    animation: act2-forest-rise var(--grow-dur, 760ms) cubic-bezier(0.16, 0.72, 0.28, 1) both;
    animation-delay: var(--flora-delay, 0ms);
  }
  /* ⚠ THE PARKED STATE FOR A SEGMENT HANGS OFF `.is-drawing`, WHICH THE SCRIPT
     STAMPS PER SEGMENT — NOT OFF `.tw-trail-fill`. A segment the schedule
     never reached would be parked by `stroke-dashoffset` FOREVER, which is a
     road missing from a map whose whole pitch is that the map is complete. An
     unscheduled segment keeps painting instead: the failure direction is a
     road that appears too early, never one that never appears. */
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-shadow.is-drawing,
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-casing.is-drawing,
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-fill.is-drawing,
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-ghost.is-drawing {
    stroke-dasharray: 1 1;
    animation: act2-trail-draw var(--draw-dur, 400ms) linear both;
    animation-delay: var(--draw-delay, 0ms);
  }
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-shadow.is-drawing.is-drawing-rev,
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-casing.is-drawing.is-drawing-rev,
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-fill.is-drawing.is-drawing-rev,
  .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-ghost.is-drawing.is-drawing-rev {
    animation-name: act2-trail-draw-rev;
  }

  /* ⚠ THE FADE IS ACQUISITION — TIME THE READER CANNOT READ THROUGH — AND act2-tell BUDGETS FOR
     IT BY NAME. `MS_BLOCK_ACQUIRE` and `MS_LINE_ACQUIRE` are these two durations transcribed, and
     `act2-tell.test.ts` reads this stylesheet and reds if they drift. A slower fade here is not a
     free polish: it is reading time taken away from every first line on the page, which is exactly
     how "couldnt read it" happened at 0.7s. */
  .tell-block {
    opacity: 0;
    translate: 0 10px;
    transition: opacity 0.34s ease, translate 0.34s ease;
  }
  .tell-block.is-on { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }
  .tell-line {
    margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.12rem, 2.5vw, 1.62rem);
    line-height: 1.36;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: #2c2318;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    opacity: 1;
    translate: 0 0;
    transition: opacity 0.3s ease, translate 0.3s ease;
  }
  .tell-line.is-hidden { opacity: 0; translate: 0 6px; }
  /* The first beat is the name, alone. It gets the size that says so. */
  .tell-layer[data-tell-beat='name'] .tell-line {
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4.4vw, 2.9rem);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .tell-figure {
    /* Sized off the diagram's own 308x250 viewBox: its four boxes are wide against its ring radius
       (it was authored for a wider embed), so the left and right nodes sit close together and the
       whole thing needs the extra scale to stay readable over a busy map. */
    width: min(25rem, 58vw);
    opacity: 0;
    translate: 0 8px;
    transition: opacity 0.7s ease, translate 0.7s ease;
  }
  .tell-figure.is-on { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }

  .tell-skip {
    margin-top: auto;
    pointer-events: auto;
    appearance: none;
    border: 1px solid rgba(74, 58, 38, 0.28);
    background: rgba(251, 243, 234, 0.72);
    color: #4a3a26;
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.95rem;
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background 0.2s ease, color 0.2s ease, border-color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .tell-skip:hover { background: rgba(251, 243, 234, 0.95); border-color: rgba(74, 58, 38, 0.5); }
  .tell-skip:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #4a3a26; outline-offset: 2px; }

  /* ---- the lenses: what a beat asks the REAL map to do while it speaks ----
         Every one of these changes how the already-rendered forest is
         displayed. None of them draws anything: `proven` reads the status
         classes the snapshot stamped, `trails` reveals edges computed from the
         corpus and hidden by default, `islands` lifts the island bodies. The
         prose points at the map; it never illustrates itself. */
  #storm-land .forest-arrival-svg .tw-isle,
  #storm-land .forest-arrival-svg .tw-trails {
    transition: opacity 0.75s ease, filter 0.75s ease;
  }
  /* ⚠ THE TRAILS LENS EMPHASISES; IT DOES NOT REVEAL. The shared stylesheet hides `.tw-trails` and
     each host opts in — but GROW already opted this surface in (see `.storm-land-canvas .tw-trails`
     above), so the edges are on screen from the moment the visitor lands. An earlier draft here
     wrote `display: inline` under the lens and read like a reveal while doing nothing at all; worse,
     it restyled the trail strokes UNCONDITIONALLY, which would have quietly changed the composition
     the owner has a look pending on. Every rule below is scoped to the lens, so the resting frame
     GROW settles on is byte-identical to what shipped. */
  #storm-land.tell-lens-trails .forest-arrival-svg .tw-trail-fill {
    stroke: rgba(88, 66, 36, 0.72);
    stroke-width: 4;
  }
  #storm-land.tell-lens-trails .forest-arrival-svg .tw-isle { opacity: 0.62; }
  #storm-land.tell-lens-islands .forest-arrival-svg .tw-isle {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 6px 14px rgba(48, 34, 18, 0.28));
  }
  /* Hold the green, let the rest fall back — the islands are all still there,
     which is the point: nothing is hidden, the proven ones are simply the ones
     being talked about. */
  #storm-land.tell-lens-proven .forest-arrival-svg .tw-isle:not(.st-healthy) {
    opacity: 0.34;
  }
  #storm-land.tell-lens-proven .forest-arrival-svg .tw-isle.st-healthy {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 6px 16px rgba(56, 92, 46, 0.4));
  }
  /* The camera has flown to one island; ring it so the sentence has a referent
     even before the reader finds it. */
  #storm-land.tell-lens-self .forest-arrival-svg .tw-isle:not([data-id='website-experience']) {
    opacity: 0.4;
  }
  #storm-land.tell-lens-self .forest-arrival-svg .tw-isle[data-id='website-experience'] {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 0 rgba(74, 58, 38, 0.9)) drop-shadow(0 8px 22px rgba(48, 34, 18, 0.42));
  }


  /* ======================================================================
     ROAM — the panel the visitor pulls open by clicking the map, and the
     invisible hit stroke that makes a trail clickable. Global because
     act2-roam.ts creates all of it at runtime; namespaced roam-.

     ⚠ NOTHING HERE APPEARS UNLESS SOMETHING WAS CLICKED. `.roam-panel` starts
     `hidden` and is only unhidden by a click; there is no timer anywhere in
     this movement. That is the whole of "we are quiet" expressed in CSS: the
     one movement that speaks is TELL, and every sentence in this panel is
     pulled rather than pushed.

     ⚠ AND NOTHING HERE CHANGES THE RESTING COMPOSITION. The owner has a look
     pending on how the forest opens; every rule below is either inside the
     panel, or scoped to `.roam-selected` — a class no element carries until a
     click puts it there. The one unscoped rule is the trail hit stroke, and it
     is TRANSPARENT.
     ====================================================================== */

  /* THE TRAIL HIT STROKE. A trail's visible line is ~3 units wide, which at the
     resting scale is roughly a CSS pixel and a half — not a thing a hand can
     hit. Rather than fatten the line (which would change the picture) or add a
     layer (which would change the DOM), this re-uses the CASING pass: the same
     routed geometry, already in the markup, currently `display: none` on this
     surface. It is painted with `transparent` and given `pointer-events:
     stroke`, so it is a wide invisible target sitting exactly under the visible
     line it belongs to. Nothing about the composition moves. */
  .storm-land-canvas .tw-trail-casing {
    display: inline;
    stroke: transparent;
    stroke-width: 18;
    opacity: 1;
    pointer-events: stroke;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* The thing the panel is talking about, ringed on the map. The ring is a
     class on an element that is already there — nothing is drawn and nothing
     moves, which is the same fence TELL's lenses keep. */
  #storm-land .forest-arrival-svg .roam-selected.tw-isle,
  #storm-land .forest-arrival-svg .roam-selected.tw-ground {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 0 rgba(74, 58, 38, 0.9)) drop-shadow(0 8px 22px rgba(48, 34, 18, 0.42));
  }
  #storm-land .forest-arrival-svg .tw-trail-fill.roam-selected {
    stroke: rgba(88, 66, 36, 0.92);
    stroke-width: 5;
  }

  .roam-panel {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 9;
    top: clamp(1rem, 4vh, 2.4rem);
    right: clamp(0.9rem, 3vw, 2.2rem);
    width: min(23rem, 42vw);
    max-height: min(82vh, 46rem);
    /* ⚠ A COLUMN WHOSE ONLY FLEXIBLE PART IS THE CAPABILITY LIST. The floor note and the snapshot
       date sit at the bottom and are the two things that must never be scrolled away — one is the
       conversion point, the other is what stops a dated export reading as a live feed. A fixed
       `max-height` on the list was tried first and does not work: it bounds the list without
       bounding everything above it, so a long title plus nine capabilities still pushed both off.
       Flex shrinks the list to whatever is left, whatever the story's size — the largest on this
       map has 27 capabilities. */
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: 1rem 1.05rem 0.85rem;
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid rgba(122, 98, 62, 0.28);
    box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(48, 34, 18, 0.16);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .roam-panel[hidden] { display: none; }

  .roam-close {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.35rem;
    right: 0.45rem;
    width: 1.7rem;
    height: 1.7rem;
    padding: 0;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .roam-close:hover { background: var(--surface-2); color: var(--ink); }

  .roam-kind {
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--amber);
  }
  .roam-title {
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
    padding-right: 1.3rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.32;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .roam-id {
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  /* One or two sentences. The size is the reading size, not a caption size —
     this is the only text on the page a visitor asked for. */
  .roam-note { margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  .roam-line {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .roam-line:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* The two rows a story panel offers: its colour, and what is inside it.
     Buttons, because they are the two further questions the visitor may ask. */
  .roam-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
    padding: 0.42rem 0.55rem;
    border: 1px solid rgba(122, 98, 62, 0.22);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--surface-2);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-align: left;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .roam-row:hover { border-color: rgba(122, 98, 62, 0.45); }
  .roam-row[aria-expanded='true'] {
    border-color: var(--amber);
    background: var(--amber-soft);
  }
  .roam-row:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--amber); outline-offset: 1px; }

  /* The status dot, in the SAME hues the island's own crown is painted with —
     the panel and the picture read one value, so they cannot disagree. */
  .roam-chip { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.42rem; }
  .roam-dot {
    width: 0.62rem;
    height: 0.62rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #9ca3af;
    flex: none;
  }
  .roam-dot.st-healthy { background: #2f6b3f; }
  .roam-dot.st-proposed { background: #b06a24; }
  .roam-dot.st-mapped { background: #7d5f3b; }
  .roam-dot.st-building { background: #4a7d8c; }
  .roam-dot.st-unhealthy { background: #9f2d22; }
  .roam-chip-word { font-weight: 550; }

  /* ⚠ THE LIST SCROLLS INSIDE ITSELF SO THE FLOOR STAYS ON SCREEN. The floor note sits BELOW this
     list and is the panel's conversion point — where the public map stops and what opens it. Left
     unbounded the list simply pushed it, and the snapshot stamp under it, past the panel's own
     max-height: both were in the DOM and neither was on screen, which a check reading textContent
     cannot tell apart from working. The largest story on this map has 27 capabilities. */
  .roam-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-height: 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
  }
  /* The one part that gives. `min-height` keeps at least a couple of rows visible so the list never
     collapses to nothing and reads as empty. */
  .roam-caps {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    padding: 0 0.3rem 0 0;
    list-style: none;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-height: 4rem;
    overflow-y: auto;
    /* A VISIBLE scrollbar, not the overlay one the engine hides. The row above says how many
       capabilities there are; without a track, a list showing two of nine looks like a list of
       two, and the panel would understate the island it is describing. */
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    scrollbar-color: rgba(122, 98, 62, 0.45) transparent;
  }
  .roam-caps::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 7px; }
  .roam-caps::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
    background: rgba(122, 98, 62, 0.45);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  /* Never shrink the two that carry the meaning. */
  .roam-note[data-roam-note='floor'] { flex: none; }
  .roam-cap {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .roam-cap .roam-dot { position: relative; top: 0.12rem; }
  .roam-cap-title { flex: 1; }

  /* Target 5 — the arc drawer. It sits one level ABOVE the code, so it reads as a block of
     initiatives rather than as a list of parts: each arc gets its title, a state word, its
     shape, and the decisions behind it. The lifecycle word is deliberately NOT a coloured dot —
     the st-* dots mean PROOF, and an arc has no verdict; borrowing that vocabulary here would
     say a green thing about something nothing ever signed. */
  .roam-arcs { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .roam-arc {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    padding-left: 0.6rem;
    border-left: 2px solid rgba(122, 98, 62, 0.28);
  }
  .roam-arc:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .roam-arc-title {
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .roam-arc-shape {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }
  .roam-arc-word {
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .roam-arc-word.lc-active { color: #7a5a1f; }
  .roam-arc-tally { color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .roam-arc-line {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .roam-arc-adr-head {
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .roam-arc-adrs { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .roam-arc-adr {
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .roam-edges { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  /* The honest tail of a truncated list. It carries a COUNT, because a list that simply stops
     looks complete — which would make the panel understate a hub, the exact opposite of what a
     dependency view is for. */
  .roam-more { margin: 0.2rem 0 0; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .roam-edge {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* The date, once, at the bottom of every panel. Without it a present-tense
     sentence about a story's state reads as a live feed, which is the single
     way a stamped snapshot backfires. */
  .roam-stamp {
    flex: none;
    margin: 0.7rem 0 0;
    padding-top: 0.55rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(122, 98, 62, 0.18);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }

  @media (max-width: 680px) {
    .roam-panel {
      top: auto;
      bottom: 3.4rem;
      left: 0.7rem;
      right: 0.7rem;
      width: auto;
      max-height: 46vh;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 680px) {
    .tell-column { max-width: 88vw; }
    .tell-figure { width: min(18rem, 78vw); }
    .tell-layer { padding: 1.2rem 1.1rem; gap: 0.7rem; }
  }

  /* ⚠ NO SEQUENCE UNDER REDUCED MOTION. act2-tell.ts renders every beat at once
     as a static column and starts no timers; this only makes sure nothing left
     over from the animated path moves. Auto-advancing text is itself a motion
     the reader asked not to have. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-isle,
    .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-ground,
    .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-terr,
    .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trails {
      animation: none; opacity: 1; transform: none; translate: 0 0;
    }
    /* The trail draw-on, unwound the same way. `mountForestGrowth` already
       arms nothing under reduced motion, so nothing should ever match this —
       it is the belt to that braces, for a preference turned on mid-run. */
    .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-shadow.is-drawing,
    .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-casing.is-drawing,
    .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-fill.is-drawing,
    .forest-arrival-svg.is-growing .tw-trail-ghost.is-drawing {
      animation: none; stroke-dasharray: none; stroke-dashoffset: 0;
    }
    .tell-layer, .tell-block, .tell-line, .tell-figure { transition: none; }
    .tell-block, .tell-line, .tell-figure { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }
    #storm-land .forest-arrival-svg .tw-isle,
    #storm-land .forest-arrival-svg .tw-trails { transition: none; }
  }

  /* ---- the COMPACT loop-diagram skin (act2-loop-diagram.ts) ---- */
  .act2-loop { margin: 0.15rem 0 0.1rem; }
  .act2-loop-svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; overflow: visible; }
  /* the connecting arcs — the loop itself */
  .act2-loop-arc {
    fill: none; stroke: #6f6450; stroke-width: 1.6; opacity: 0.85;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 3;
    animation: act2-loop-flow 0.9s linear infinite;
  }
  .act2-loop-arrowhead { fill: #8f846a; }
  @keyframes act2-loop-flow { to { stroke-dashoffset: -6; } }
  /* the node boxes */
  .act2-loop-box {
    fill: #23201a; stroke: #453c29; stroke-width: 1.2;
  }
  .act2-loop-label {
    fill: #e7debd; font: 650 7.9px 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace;
    letter-spacing: -0.2px;
  }
  .act2-loop-who {
    fill: #8f846a; font: 600 6.6px 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace;
    letter-spacing: 0.1px; text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  /* an agent writes (author / build) — warm sage, the "someone is doing work" tone */
  .act2-loop-node.kind-author .act2-loop-box,
  .act2-loop-node.kind-build .act2-loop-box {
    fill: #232a1f; stroke: #4c5a3a;
  }
  .act2-loop-node.kind-author .act2-loop-who,
  .act2-loop-node.kind-build .act2-loop-who { fill: #8fb87a; }
  /* the SYSTEM checks (referee) — a distinct cool steel, unmistakably NOT an agent */
  .act2-loop-node.kind-check .act2-loop-box {
    fill: #1c2732; stroke: #3a5064;
  }
  .act2-loop-node.kind-check .act2-loop-who { fill: #8fbcd6; }
  .act2-loop-node.kind-check .act2-loop-label { fill: #eaf2f8; }
  /* the centre caption — the referee, named */
  .act2-loop-centre {
    fill: #b9ad90; font: 600 7.4px 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace;
  }
  .act2-loop-centre-em { fill: #8fbcd6; font-weight: 700; }
  /* scaffolded reveal: each node fades/grows in (the arcs stay so the loop reads) */
  .act2-loop-node {
    transform-box: fill-box; transform-origin: center;
    transition: opacity 0.34s ease;
  }
  .act2-loop-node.is-hidden { opacity: 0; }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .act2-loop-arc { animation: none; }
    .act2-loop-node { transition: none; }
    .act2-loop-node.is-hidden { opacity: 1; }
  }

  /* ---- small screens: same cap, stacked windows ---- */
  @media (max-width: 680px) {
    .storm-hud { padding: 8px 10px; gap: 8px; font-size: 10.5px; }
    .storm-count { font-size: 11.5px; }
    .storm-term { left: 50% !important; translate: -50% 0; width: min(420px, 88vw) !important; }
    .storm-boot { top: 40%; translate: -50% -50%; }
    .storm-boot .term-body { font-size: 12px; }
    /* the finale keeps its center + width on small screens (more specific than
       the generic .storm-term stack rule above) */
    .storm-term.storm-finale {
      top: 50%;
      translate: -50% -50%;
      width: min(640px, 92vw) !important;
      max-height: 78vh;
    }
    .storm-finale .term-body { min-height: 150px; font-size: 12px; }
  }


  /* ======================================================================
     Act 2 — the guided walk on the REAL 2.5D map (ADR-0145). Global because
     the stage / callout DOM is created at runtime by act2-walkthrough.ts.
     The MAP itself is styled by TreeWorld.astro's tw-* vocabulary (always on
     the page); only the walk chrome (stage, callout, inspect) lives here,
     namespaced act2-. (The wrong-way ghost pads + violation flag are RETIRED —
     ADR-0150 §4 / ADR-0153. The corner overlays went with ADR-0165 §2, and the
     guide chat, growing diagram, mini-map and studio layer went with the
     narrator's retirement — `website-refresh-arc-pitch-overlays`. The walk
     itself is unmounted but NOT retired: its disc geometry still draws the real
     forest, so it outlives its own chrome.)
     ====================================================================== */

  /* ---- the stage: fades up over the R3F canvas at begin ---- */
  .act2-stage {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 2;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.9s ease;
    background: radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 38%, #fbf3ea 0%, #f3e3d2 55%, #edd9c9 100%);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
  }
  .act2-stage.is-live { opacity: 1; }
  .storm-land.act2-cut .act2-stage { transition: none; }
  .act2-canvas { position: absolute; inset: 0; }
  .act2-stage .tw-svg { width: 100%; height: 100%; display: block; }
  /* the stage's own CSS gradient is the board — the scene's bg rect would read
     as a pale seam once the pull-back viewBox grows past the 1400×1200 scene
     frame (the visible-band camera zooms wider than the landed build did) */
  .act2-stage .act2-bg { fill: none; }
  /* the walk is a diorama, not the studio: the delegation hit layer is inert */
  .act2-stage .tw-hit { pointer-events: none; cursor: default; }

  /* ---- the ORBITING wisp (ADR-0165 §5, replacing the landed act2-wisp-drift;
          ADR-0157 §6's "the wisp moves" now has its approved specific motion):
          the walkthrough post-processes the wisp layer into
            .act2-orbit-plane (translate to the tree spot + scale(1, 0.55) — the
              flattening that makes the circle read as an ellipse lying on the
              2.5D map)
            └ .act2-orbit-spin (THIS rotates — one lap ≈ 9 s, pure CSS; an
                invisible symmetric extent circle keeps its fill-box origin at
                the true centre)
              └ the faint dashed orbit ring + the scene's own translate(orbitR)
                carrier holding the wisp.
          The shared map CSS spins .tw-wisp about its own centre (tw-orbit) —
          neutralised here; the glow pulse is kept. Fixed initial angle, no
          randomness — byte-identical on Back replay. Reduced motion: no
          rotation (the wisp rests at a fixed orbit point), pulse only. Motion
          is site-side; the wisp presence marker stays the director's scene
          semantics (the ADR-0145 boundary). ---- */
  .act2-stage .tw-wisp {
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: center;
    animation: none; /* the orbit rides the wrapper, not the wisp group */
  }
  .act2-stage .act2-orbit-spin {
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: center;
    animation: act2-orbit 9s linear infinite;
  }
  @keyframes act2-orbit {
    to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
  }
  .act2-stage .tw-wisp .glow { animation: act2-wisp-glow 3.4s ease-in-out infinite; }
  @keyframes act2-wisp-glow {
    0%, 100% { opacity: 0.22; }
    50%      { opacity: 0.5; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .act2-stage .act2-orbit-spin,
    .act2-stage .tw-wisp .glow { animation: none; }
  }
  /* before beat 1 the land is empty — the synthetic territory's tree/plate/hit
     stay out of the render */
  .act2-svg.is-prestory .tw-flora-layer,
  .act2-svg.is-prestory .tw-hits { display: none; }
  /* an inspectable proposed upstream tree (UAT 5) — a soft affordance cue */
  .act2-stage .tw-terr.act2-inspectable { cursor: pointer; }
  .act2-stage .tw-terr.act2-inspectable:hover .tw-plate .bg,
  .act2-stage .tw-terr.act2-inspectable:focus-visible .tw-plate .bg {
    stroke: var(--evergreen); stroke-width: 1.4;
  }
  .act2-stage .tw-terr.act2-inspectable:focus-visible { outline: none; }

  /* ---- growth: what a beat ADDS scales/fades in (deterministic classes;
          scale only ever rides transform-attribute-free groups) ---- */
  .act2-enter {
    transform-box: fill-box; transform-origin: center;
    animation: act2-grow 0.8s cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.4, 0.44, 1) both;
    animation-delay: calc(var(--ei, 0) * 0.22s);
  }
  .act2-enter-fade {
    animation: act2-fade 0.9s ease-out both;
    animation-delay: calc(var(--ei, 0) * 0.3s);
  }
  @keyframes act2-grow { from { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.55); } to { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); } }
  @keyframes act2-fade { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }

  /* ---- the dependency trails: a clean "NEEDS" edge from the dependent DOWN to
          its prerequisite (ADR-0153 §1; the wrong-way-flag teach is RETIRED —
          the honest dependency layers ARE the teach, ADR-0150 §4). Trails are
          HIDDEN BY DEFAULT on the public map (ADR-0169 §3/§4) — this beat
          narratively SHOWS them, so the diorama container opts back in with a
          scoped display override. The routed geometry + widths ride the engine's
          path attributes (the shared width rule); this restyles colour/dash only. ---- */
  .act2-stage .tw-trails { display: inline; }
  .act2-stage .tw-trail-shadow, .act2-stage .tw-trail-casing { display: none; }
  .act2-stage .tw-trail-fill {
    stroke: var(--evergreen); stroke-width: 2.2; opacity: 0.85;
    stroke-dasharray: 6 5;
    animation: act2-needs 1.4s linear infinite;
  }
  @keyframes act2-needs { to { stroke-dashoffset: -11; } }

  /* ---- the BaaS DIRECT READ edge (ADR-0157 §1): the website reads its catalog
          straight from the database. The engine routes it AROUND the backend
          island (island avoidance, ADR-0169 §1); here it reads as a DISTINCT
          lighter, finer, quicker-dashed curve — "a direct read", not the heavier
          "needs" edge — so a newcomer sees the website both reads the catalog
          straight from the database AND goes through the backend for checkout.
          Keyed by the segments' edge keys (data-edges carries `from->to`). ---- */
  .act2-stage .tw-trail-fill[data-edges*="story-checkout->story-database"] {
    stroke: var(--amber); stroke-width: 1.8; opacity: 0.9;
    stroke-dasharray: 3 4;
    animation: act2-reads 1.1s linear infinite;
  }
  @keyframes act2-reads { to { stroke-dashoffset: -7; } }

  /* ---- the anchored callout (the game-tutorial box, tail at its anchor) ---- */
  .act2-callout {
    position: absolute; z-index: 6;
    width: min(340px, 84vw);
    background: var(--surface); color: var(--ink);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    box-shadow: 0 16px 48px rgba(40, 26, 12, 0.28);
    padding: 0.95rem 1.1rem 0.9rem;
    opacity: 0; translate: 0 6px; pointer-events: none;
    transition: opacity 0.28s ease, translate 0.28s ease;
  }
  .act2-callout.is-placed { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; pointer-events: auto; }
  .act2-callout[hidden] { display: none; }
  .act2-tail {
    position: absolute; width: 14px; height: 14px;
    background: var(--surface); rotate: 45deg;
  }
  .act2-head { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.6rem; margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
  .act2-step {
    margin: 0; font: 650 0.66rem var(--font-body); letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--amber);
  }
  .act2-title { margin: 0 0 0.3rem; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.12rem; color: var(--evergreen); }
  .act2-body { margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .act2-legend {
    list-style: none; display: grid; gap: 0.35rem;
    margin: 0.65rem 0 0; padding: 0.65rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  /* display:grid would defeat the UA's [hidden] rule — the legend shows ONLY
     on the pull-back beat */
  .act2-legend[hidden] { display: none; }
  .act2-legend li { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .act2-legend .k { width: 0.65rem; height: 0.65rem; border-radius: 999px; flex: none; }
  .act2-legend .k-green { background: var(--evergreen); }
  .act2-legend .k-pale { background: var(--amber); }
  .act2-legend .k-with { background: var(--crimson); }
  /* (the callout's Next/Back controls + progress dots are RETIRED — ADR-0165
     §3: the chat chips advance, the chat's quiet back replays; the callout is
     a pure anchored pointer) */

  /* ---- the inline inspect prompt (UAT 5 — open a proposed upstream story) ---- */
  .act2-inspect-open {
    display: block; margin: 0.7rem 0 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--evergreen); border-radius: 999px; cursor: pointer;
    background: var(--evergreen-soft); color: var(--evergreen-deep);
    font: 600 0.82rem var(--font-body);
    padding: 0.4rem 0.8rem; text-align: left; width: 100%;
  }
  .act2-inspect-open[hidden] { display: none; }
  .act2-inspect-open:hover { background: #dcebd9; }

  /* ---- the inspect panel: what a proposed upstream story is + why (UAT 5) ---- */
  .act2-inspect {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 9;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    padding: 1.2rem;
    background: rgba(30, 22, 12, 0.28);
    opacity: 0; pointer-events: none;
    transition: opacity 0.24s ease;
  }
  .act2-inspect.is-open { opacity: 1; pointer-events: auto; }
  .act2-inspect[hidden] { display: none; }
  .act2-inspect-card {
    width: min(430px, 92vw);
    background: var(--surface); color: var(--ink);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    box-shadow: 0 24px 80px rgba(40, 26, 12, 0.4);
    padding: 1.3rem 1.4rem 1.2rem;
    translate: 0 8px; transition: translate 0.24s ease;
  }
  .act2-inspect.is-open .act2-inspect-card { translate: 0 0; }
  .act2-inspect-badge {
    display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    font: 700 0.62rem var(--font-body); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--amber); background: var(--amber-soft);
    padding: 0.26rem 0.55rem; border-radius: 999px;
  }
  .act2-inspect-title { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--evergreen); }
  .act2-inspect-h {
    margin: 0.7rem 0 0.15rem; font: 700 0.7rem var(--font-body);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
  .act2-inspect-p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .act2-inspect-close {
    margin-top: 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--line-strong); border-radius: 8px; cursor: pointer;
    background: var(--surface-2); color: var(--ink);
    font: 600 0.82rem var(--font-body); padding: 0.45rem 0.95rem;
  }
  .act2-inspect-close:hover { background: var(--surface); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .act2-inspect, .act2-inspect-card { transition: none; }
    .act2-inspect.is-open .act2-inspect-card { translate: 0 0; }
  }

  /* ---- the honest diorama-closing CTA — centred in the VISIBLE band above
